लिए ground-truth derivation लिखिए।\\n3. Controls: चारों जो आपने गिनाए — (i) no-planted-pattern negative control से false-rare-rate, (ii) random-score baseline, (iii) trivial frequency-count baseline, (iv) threshold sensitivity sweep — plus एक और: category-collapse/marginal-skew control, ताकि यह पता चले कि मिलने वाला \\\"rare pattern\\\" असली interaction है या केवल skewed marginal distribution का artifact। यह अंतिम control इस प्रश्न के लिए सबसे महत्वपूर्ण है।\\n4. Analysis plan: precision@k / recall, support-threshold के विरुद्ध rare-pattern recall curve का area, resample stability; और combination-level rarity के लिए एक multiple-comparisons/subset-search correction — कम-आवृत्ति itemset enumeration में sheer combinatorial volume के कारण假 rarity कैसे नियंत्रित होगी।\\n5. Feasibility: execution-required नहीं; केवल इतना लिखिए कि वास्तविक run के लिए क्या-क्या चाहिए (Docta source, numpy/pandas, data file), और current sandbox में वह अनुपलब्ध क्यों है।\\n\\nकोई भी संख्या, कोई \\\"हमने चलाकर देखा\\\" जैसा कथन, या कोई ऐसा Docta API symbol मत दीजिए जिसे आपने inspect नहीं किया। हर setup item को [inspected] या [assumption] label दीजिए। यदि कोई चीज़ वास्तव में अखुली रहे तो उसे unresolved uncertainty के रूप में रखिए — कृपना से अनुमान मत भरिए।\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "e7396f12-9170-4e78-a4c2-f6a7103ba840", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T15:00:05.885523", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "60b36c08-7d6d-40a5-98e9-b03f91977d68", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "All required elements are specified; no further inspection is needed (the environment state was fully characterized last turn and cannot change without network). Submitting the complete two-branch design."}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher has approved the plan and is asking for the full stage=result design under the following conditions:\n\n1. A proper rarity-definition hierarchy: point / combination / segment / rule levels. With operational definitions and threshold notation (t, k, size floor).\n2. Dataset spec: synthetic table spec for planted rarity + one named public dataset; ground-truth derivation for both.\n3. Controls: the 5 enumerated controls including a category-collapse / marginal-distribution-skew control.\n4. Analysis plan: precision@k / recall, area under the recall curve against support threshold, resample stability, multiple-comparison / subset-search correction for combinatorial false rarity.\n5. Feasibility: design only; what is needed for actual execution; why the sandbox cannot do it.\n\nNo fabricated numbers, no claims of execution, no fabricated Docta symbols. Label everything with [inspected] / [assumption]. Retain unresolved uncertainties.\n\nDataset candidates should be chosen carefully. Named public datasets with inherent rarity ground truth:\n- Adult Census Income (UCI) — but the rarity ground truth is annotation-based; can use rare attribute combinations.\n- Better: Covertype (UCI) — class 5 (Spruce/Fir) is about 1.9% of rows, providing natural class-level rarity; also many feature combinations.\n- Classic options for anomaly detection: UCI KDD Cup 1999 (attack types = rare classes), or electricity/forestcover. Forest CoverType is a good choice: named (Blackard 1999, UCI), ~581k rows × 54 cols, minority class ~1.9%. But it's large; note the feasibility.\n- Or the \"Intrusion\" subset of UCI KDD Cup 99, where U2R/R2L attacks are rare (less than about 1%). Named and established in anomaly-detection literature.\n- The Zipfian distribution of the UCI \"Mushroom\" dataset? Hmm.\n- Another good candidate: Fashion-MNIST is not tabular. \"Adult\" dataset with rare combos like (occupation=machine-inspectors, capital-gain>50k...).\n\nAdopt Forest CoverType as primary (documented minority classes: class 5 Krummib/Whitebark pine ~0.5%, class 7 willow/dry meadow ~1.9%? From memory: covertype distribution: class1 36.6%, class2 45.8%, class3 6.3%, class4 0.94%, class5 1.9%, class6 2.8%, class7 5.4%). So class 4 (Krummib) ~0.47%, class 5 ~1.9% — good rare-segment targets. But this is a fairly large memory footprint; note it. Or use the smaller (100k rows) UCI \"letter\" dataset. Or electricity. Make Covertype the canonical validation dataset, with [assumption] labels on exact proportions, and state that they will be recomputed rather than cited at execution time — good practice: derive the ground truth rather than cite it.\n\nSynthetic spec: N=10,000 rows [assumption chosen value]; columns:\n- x1..x5: numeric, standard normal + correlated blocks (specify correlation structure)\n- c1..c4: categorical with skewed marginals (specify cardinality and Dirichlet-ish probabilities)\n- Binary flags\nPlanted patterns (ground truth by construction):\n- P1 point anomalies: 20 rows (0.2%) drawn from shifted Gaussian N(mu=6,1) in a single dimension; additionally 20 rows on a shifted 2D subspace.\n- P2 combination: rows where (c1=A & c2=B & x3<q05) hold, planted at 0.5% base rate.\n- P3 segment/cluster: a compact cluster of 30 rows (0.3%) at a specified location.\n- P4 rule: a low-support high-lift implication (c3=X ∧ x2 in band) ⇒ y=1, support 0.4%, lift 8.\nMarginal-distribution skew control: make individual marginals of the rare combo extremely common (each marginal ≥ 20%). This ensures that the combination's rarity is purely due to interaction. Also, plant a decoy rare pattern that exists only as a product of marginals: design two categories that are each rare on their own, independently occurring at the product rate — the \"independence decoy\" — which any reasonable analysis should not report as an interesting rare pattern; plus a decoy pattern embedded in a skewed-marginal region (decoy \"pseudo-interaction\") which the category-collapse control should reject.\n\nThreshold notation:\n- Support s(P) = fraction of rows matching pattern P. Rare threshold t (e.g., t=0.01), swept over {0.001..0.05}.\n- Point level: anomaly score a(x), top-k set R_k = top-k by score; k set from planted count.\n- Segment: clustering; rare cluster = size < n_min (size floor, e.g., 0.5% of N).\n- Rule level: support < t_rule and lift > L_min; also conviction.\n- Family-wise correction over subset search: for itemsets of size ≤ d, number of candidate itemsets M = sum over categorical combos; Bonferroni/Holm at item-level alpha; or permutation null: permute columns independently and compute minimum support for each itemset to build a null distribution — a max-statistic permutation procedure controlling family-wise error (Westfall–Young). This handles combinatorial false rarity.\n\nMetric bindings:\n- Point: precision@k (k = planted count), recall@k, AUPRC of score vs binary rare label.\n- Combination: recall on planted combos at threshold t; area under recall-vs-t curve over sweep; F1 of discovered rare itemsets vs planted.\n- Segment: cluster-to-planted-cluster adjusted Rand / purity; recall = fraction of planted segment members inside rare clusters.\n- Rule: precision/recall of recovered rules after exact-match + subsumption handling.\n- Stability: 10 bootstrap resamples, Jaccard of discovery set; report planned statistic.\n\nCategory-collapse / marginal-distribution-skew control: for each discovered rare combination, refit by collapsing each constituent to the complement; compute whether rarity is explained by marginal rates (independence expected support = product of marginals; report the ratio s_obs / s_indep). A \"true interaction\" requires s_obs << s_indep AND each marginal above a floor (e.g., each marginal ≥ 0.05). Also, re-run detection after re-weighting to uniform marginals (importance-weighted resampling), and require the pattern to survive → genuine interaction, not a marginal-distribution-skew artifact. Plus a planted independence decoy: patterns whose observed support equals the product of marginals should not be flagged (expected false-precision cost).\n\nFeasibility: what is needed for actual execution: Docta source/package (unobtainable — network disabled [inspected]), numpy/pandas/sklearn (not present [inspected]), data files (Covertype is not in the datasets list [inspected]). Sandbox: no network, no science stack — hence design only, consistent with task.json's track.md.\n\nTwo-branch structure:\n- PRIMARY: Docta = documentation/automation layer (explicitly stated as an assumption; premise revision). Detection performed by standard primitives; Docta packages notebooks + configs + figures into reproducible docs (per prior knowledge, the notebook-based workflow is actually what Docta consumes — label as assumption).\n- CONDITIONAL appendix: an API-agnostic interface for 4 primitive slots: score_rank(x)->scores, density_estimate(X)->scores, enumerate_itemsets(min_support)->supports, cluster_assignments()->labels+sizes. A mapping table of which method slots each rarity level depends on; fallback if primitives are absent; no fabricated symbol names.\n\nAlso, define the detection algorithms in the primary branch generically: rank by k-NN distance / LOF-analog; Isolation-Forest-analog; frequency counting; k-means/DBSCAN-analog; association-rule mining. Since sklearn is an assumption, describe as method families.\n\nEverything labeled with [inspected] / [assumption]. Uncertainties: Docta identity; Covertype exact class proportions to be recomputed at execution time; threshold defaults are starting points rather than tuned; etc.\n\nLength: the answer should be a full design. Write carefully and efficiently. Also, do I need to re-execute something? Not really, but could do one simple check — no; inspection was completed in the previous turn. Just submit the result.\n\nFormat: structured markdown inside an \"answer\" field. It will be long, but that's fine. Keep evidence with inspection citations. Rationale/controls/analysis/feasibility inside the answer per instructions (\"complete design in answer, backed by rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasible checks, repository observations in evidence\") — place the design fully in answer, and put supporting repository observations in evidence.\n\nNow draft.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Design: Identifying Rare Patterns in a Tabular Dataset Using Docta\n\n## 0. Premise handling (fixed by researcher decision)\n- **Docta identity = UNKNOWN.** [inspected] No package, source, repo, or API named \"Docta\" exists in this sandbox (filesystem-wide search; `import docta` → ModuleNotFoundError; `pip3 show docta` → not found; only occurrence of the string is task.json's question text). No Docta symbol names appear anywhere below.\n- **PRIMARY branch (main design):** [assumption] Docta functions as a **documentation/automation layer**. Detection is performed by standard method families; Docta's role is packaging the workflow (notebook + config + figures + method text) into a reproducible, browsable analysis record. The original premise \"detect *with* Docta\" is recorded as a **corrected premise**: rare patterns are detected by standard primitives and *documented/reproduced* via Docta.\n- **CONDITIONAL appendix (§8):** if Docta turns out to be an analytics package, a primitive-slot interface maps each detection step onto score/rank, density, itemset, and cluster slots without inventing symbols.\n\n## 1. Canonical rarity-definition hierarchy (all metrics in §6 bind to these)\nLet D be a table with n rows, columns X = numeric ∪ categorical. **Support** s(P) = fraction of rows matching pattern P.\n\n1. **Point level — rare row.** A row x whose density under the empirical data model is low: anomaly score a(x) in the top-k tail. Operational set: R_k = k rows with highest a(x). Thresholds: **k** (budget), tied to planted count where known.\n2. **Combination level — low-support itemset.** A conjunction P of per-column predicates (categorical equalities, numeric interval bins). P is *rare* iff s(P) < **t**. Enumeration restricted to conjunction width ≤ **d** (search-depth cap).\n3. **Segment level — small cluster.** Partition via a clustering method family; cluster C is rare iff |C| < **n_min** (size floor). Report the row-set of every rare cluster.\n4. **Rule level — low-support/high-lift rule.** An implication A ⇒ B is a *rare rule* iff s(A∪B) < **t_rule** and lift = s(A∪B)/(s(A)s(B)) > **L_min** (also report conviction). Rules are combinations with a consequent; they inherit t and d.\n\nNotation fixed once: t (support threshold), k (point budget), n_min (size floor), d (max conjunction width), L_min (lift floor). §7 sweeps t, k, n_min; defaults are starting points [assumption], not tuned values.\n\n## 2. Dataset specification and ground-truth derivation\n### 2a. Synthetic planted-rarity table (primary evaluation vehicle) — all values [assumption], chosen for the design\n- **N = 10,000 rows.** Columns: `x1..x5` numeric ~ N(0,1) with two correlated pairs (corr 0.7 between x1–x2, x3–x4); `c1..c4` categorical with cardinalities 4/6/5/8 and deliberately **skewed marginals** (top-category probability 0.35–0.60); `y` binary target. No missing values in v1.\n- **Planted patterns (ground truth = construction mask, generated alongside rows):**\n  - **G1 point anomalies (n=20, 0.2%):** 10 rows with x5 ~ N(6,1) (univariate); 10 rows with (x1,x3) ~ N((5,5),I) (bivariate).\n  - **G2 rare combination (n≈50, 0.5%):** rows satisfying (c1=cat_A ∧ c2=cat_B ∧ x3 < q₀.₀₅(x3)), with each *individual* marginal ≥ 0.20 by construction — combination rarity is pure interaction (this construction *is* the check the §3 control probes).\n  - **G3 rare segment (n=30, 0.3%):** compact cluster at (x1..x4)=(2,2,−2,−2) with σ=0.3.\n  - **G4 rare rule (support ≈0.4%, lift ≈8):** (c3=val_X ∧ x2 ∈ [1.5,2.5]) ⇒ y=1.\n  - **D1 independence decoy:** (c4=a ∧ c1=B) planted at exactly the product of its marginals — *expected support equals independent support*; a correct method should not report it as interesting rarity.\n  - **D2 marginal-skew decoy:** (c2=p) alone has support 0.6% and G2's categories are correlated with the skew direction — patterns whose rarity vanishes under marginal reweighting must be rejected.\n- **Ground-truth derivation:** every planted row carries a silent `planted_id` column excluded from detection input; per-pattern truth sets G1..G4, D1, D2 are defined by construction, not annotation.\n\n### 2b. Public-dataset validation — UCI Forest CoverType [assumption: dataset identity from prior knowledge; file must be fetched at a real run; it is NOT present here — inspected]\n- ~581k rows × 54 columns (10 continuous, 44 binary wilderness-area/soil-type indicators) — naturally contains rare segments: minority cover classes plus rare wilderness-area × elevation-band combinations. Exact class proportions are **not quoted** here; they will be *recomputed from the fetched file* at run time (no memorized numbers).\n- **Ground-truth derivation:** (i) segment-level truth = membership in the two smallest cover classes; (ii) combination-level truth = rare binary-area × binned-elevation conjunctions with s < t; (iii) no point-level truth exists, so point methods are evaluated only by agreement with the synthetic benchmark's score-ranking behavior and by inspection-annotated extremes. This asymmetry is a stated limitation, not hidden.\n\n## 3. Controls (all five)\n1. **No-planted-pattern negative control:** identical table with G1–G4/D1/D2 removed (pure baseline generation). Measure **false-rare-rate**: fraction of flagged rows/itemsets/rules at each (t, k, n_min) — upper-bounds Type-I behavior.\n2. **Random-score baseline:** uniform-random anomaly scores and random cluster assignments; precision@k and recall@k for every rarity level must beat this for a method to count as informative.\n3. **Trivial frequency-count baseline:** flag rarest single-column values / raw itemsets with no interaction modeling, no density model, no clustering. Docta-packaged method must show *incremental value* over this baseline (this also directly answers \"is sophisticated machinery needed?\").\n4. **Threshold sensitivity sweep:** t ∈ {0.001, 0.005, 0.01, 0.02, 0.05}; k ∈ {20, 50, 100}; n_min ∈ {0.1%, 0.5%, 1%} × N; d ∈ {2,3,4}. Report all metric curves vs thresholds (no cherry-picked operating point).\n5. **Category-collapse / marginal-skew control (most important for this question):** for every candidate rare pattern:\n   - compute **interaction ratio** ρ = s_obs / s_indep, where s_indep = ∏ marginal supports of constituent predicates;\n   - require every constituent marginal ≥ 0.05 and ρ ≤ ρ_max (e.g., 0.25) to call it a genuine interaction;\n   - **marginal-reweighting re-test:** importance-resample rows to uniform category marginals and re-detect; a genuine interaction survives, a skew artifact disappears;\n   - **decoy checks:** D1 must NOT be promoted (ρ ≈ 1 by construction), D2 must be rejected by the reweighting test. Pass/fail on D1/D2 is the headline validity gate for the whole design — it distinguishes real rare interactions from marginal-distribution artifacts.\n\n## 4. Detection procedure (PRIMARY branch — method families, not library symbols)\nPer rarity level, executed as one documented notebook per level:\n- **Point:** k-NN-distance and local-density-ratio scoring (LOF-family) plus isolation-style ensemble scoring (Isolation-Forest-family); rank rows, take top-k.\n- **Combination:** discretize numerics into quantile bins; enumerate conjunctions width ≤ d with a bottom-up closed-itemset enumeration collecting supports; keep s < t.\n- **Segment:** centroid clustering (k-means-family) and density-connectivity clustering (DBSCAN-family) with size distribution; flag clusters under n_min.\n- **Rule:** association-rule generation over the low-support region (rare-rule mining: enumerate low-support itemsets first, then derive implications with lift/conviction filters).\n- **Docta role (documentation layer, [assumption] about its function):** each notebook, its config (thresholds, seed, dataset hash), the planted-pattern generator, and the metric figures are declared in the project's doc configuration and rendered into one reproducible report; the exact Docta entry points are left as `<to be filled from Docta source when available>` — no symbols invented.\n\n## 5. Rationale\n- Planted ground truth makes the question *answerable* — public datasets lack row-level rarity truth, so the synthetic table carries the evaluation while CoverType tests transfer to real skew/correlation structure.\n- The five controls map one-to-one onto the five ways rare-pattern mining lies: chance flags (controls 1–2), unnecessary complexity (3), threshold fishing (4), and marginal-skew artifacts (5).\n- The corrected premise is honest and testable: the design still fully answers \"how to identify rare patterns,\" and Docta's documented contribution (reproducibility of the analysis record) is assessed by whether the report regenerates end-to-end from config + data hash.\n\n## 6. Analysis plan (pre-registered metrics, bound to §1 notation)\n- **Point:** precision@k, recall@k against G1; AUPRC of score vs planted binary; per family (univariate vs bivariate G1 subsets — exposes univariate-score blindness).\n- **Combination:** recall of planted G2 (as itemsets/subsumers) vs t; **area under the rare-pattern recall curve** over the §3.4 sweep (AURPC); F1 over {planted G2} ∪ decoys, where D1 counted as a false positive.\n- **Segment:** recall = fraction of G3 rows inside rare clusters; adjusted Rand restricted to G3 ∪ sampled controls; cluster-size distribution plots vs n_min.\n- **Rule:** exact/near match of G4 antecedent–consequent (allowing equivalent logical restatements); precision across all mined rules.\n- **Resample stability:** 10 bootstrap resamples (B fixed at design time); Jaccard similarity of each level's discovered set vs full-data set; report median and worst-case — stability gate before any discovery claim.\n- **Combinatorial false-rarity correction (critical for combination level):** with n columns and width ≤ d, candidate count M grows combinatorially, so minimum-support hits arise by sheer search volume. Correction: Westfall–Young **max-statistic permutation** — permute each column independently B=1000 times, per replicate compute the standardized support deficit of every candidate itemset, build the null of the *maximum* statistic, and accept itemsets only below Holm-adjusted α=0.05. Report accepted set with and without correction to quantify how much \"rarity\" was enumeration artifact. This operates jointly with the ρ filter of control 5 (permutation handles multiplicity; ρ handles interaction vs skew).\n\n## 7. Threshold sensitivity is folded into §3.4 and reported as curves, not single numbers (see §6 AURPC).\n\n## 8. CONDITIONAL appendix — API-agnostic primitive slots (only if Docta proves to be an analytics package)\nFour slots; the §4 pipeline depends on nothing else:\n- `slot SCORE_RANK(rows) -> per-row real score` ← replaces point-level scorer. Fallback if absent: keep method families.\n- `slot DENSITY(rows) -> per-row density estimate` ← feeds both point scoring and DBSCAN-family sizing.\n- `slot ITEMSET_SUPPORT(D, max_width d) -> (itemset, support) table` ← replaces §4 combination enumeration; §6 permutation null unchanged (it wraps the slot).\n- `slot CLUSTER(D) -> (label, size)` ← replaces segment step.\nMapping: point→SCORE_RANK(+DENSITY); combination→ITEMSET_SUPPORT; segment→CLUSTER; rule→ITEMSET_SUPPORT + deterministic implication post-processing (post-processing never delegated, so rule semantics stay inspectable). Acceptance criterion for a real Docta: each slot's output must be reproducible at fixed seed and expose enough information (raw scores/supports, not just flags) to permit the §3 controls and §6 permutation null. If a slot returns pre-thresholded flags only, thresholds become unauditable and that finding itself is reported.\n\n## 9. Feasibility (design-only; nothing executed)\n- Current sandbox **cannot** run this: network disabled, Docta absent, numpy/pandas/scikit-learn absent, no datasets present — all [inspected] last turn. Per task.json (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\") the deliverable is this document.\n- A real execution would need: (1) a verifiable Docta source/package once network exists (to fill §4's placeholder and test the PRIMARY branch's packaging role); (2) numpy/pandas + one implementation each of the four method families; (3) the CoverType CSV from the UCI repository (~250 MB, CPU-only, minutes-scale; 224 CPUs available [inspected], no GPU needed); (4) the synthetic generator (self-contained, ~100 lines, seeds fixed).\n- No runtime constraint binds the design itself [assumption].\n\n## 10. Labels and unresolved uncertainties\nFixed requirements: design-only, two-branch structure, Docta identity unknown, no fabricated symbols — all honored. **[inspected]:** absence of Docta and of all scientific packages and datasets; sandbox constraints. **[assumption]:** Docta-as-documentation-layer function; all synthetic parameters (N, columns, planted rates, decoys); CoverType identity/proportions (to be recomputed, not quoted); default t/k/n_min/d/L_min values; method-family availability at a future run. **Unresolved (deliberately not guessed):** Docta's